# wy/wyrm/compiler/statements.wy - epic 10 M3
#
# Wyrm port of pypoc/wypoc/compiler_bc/statements.py: statement lowering
# (spec 7.2).
#
# Statements are compiled in two passes over a body: something upstream of
# this file (a function's prologue, M4's `functions.wy`, or M6's
# `module.wy` for the top level) walks the body first with
# `analysis.wy`'s `own_declared_names`/`FnContext.declare`/
# `FnContext.allocate_block_scopes` and gives every name it introduces a
# home - an L slot inside a function, a module global slot at the top
# level - before `compile_body` below emits a single instruction. The order
# matters: a local declared after temps had been allocated would land on
# one (context.wy's `reserve_local` refuses exactly this). `compile_body`
# itself does not do that walk (see deviation 1) - it assumes it has
# already happened, the same assumption statements.py's `compile_body`
# makes of whatever called it.
#
# Statements have values. A function's value is its last statement's, so
# every handler takes a `result` register, which is non-nil only for the
# statement in that position. A construct that produces no value of its own
# gets a `lnil` into it instead, which is what the interpreter answers for
# a loop, a declaration, or an `if` whose condition was false.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deviations from statements.py, each forced by the port
#
# 1. `declare_names` names a *shape*, not a function. statements.py's own
#    module docstring says "declare_names walks a body first"; there is no
#    such function anywhere in statements.py - what actually does the
#    walking is `analysis.py`'s `own_declared_names` plus
#    `FnContext.declare`/`allocate_block_scopes`, driven once per function
#    from `functions.py`'s prologue (not ported until M4) or, at the top
#    level, from `module.py`'s init-code walk (M6). So `compile_body` here,
#    like compile_body there, only emits code; it never declares a name.
#    The test driver below does that walk by hand, the way M2's driver
#    stubbed a function context, standing in for M4/M6's future prologue.
#
# 2. No position tracking, so no line table. pypoc's `compile_statement`
#    stamps `fn.line` from `node.pos` before dispatching, which is what
#    feeds the debug line table (spec 4.10). wy/wyrm's parser attaches no
#    position to any node (see `handlers.wy`'s header, deviation 3), and
#    `FnContext.line` is consequently unwritten by any milestone so far
#    (M1's own port of `emit_words` reads it but nothing sets it). This
#    file does not set it either: a real front-end gap, not something to
#    paper over here.
#
# 3. Dispatch goes through `handlers.wy`'s registries, not an `elif` chain.
#    `expressions.wy` dispatches expressions with its own hand-written `if
#    ks == "str": ... elif ...` inside `compile_expr` (M2's idiom, and nothing
#    here touches it - see the `_do` note below for the one exception this
#    milestone makes). This file's own entry points, `compile_statement`
#    and the constructs `handlers.wy`'s `dispatch` reaches, use the registry
#    this milestone was asked to port instead: `STATEMENT_HANDLERS` (plus
#    `EXPR_HANDLERS["if"]`, wired but not yet reachable - see the `_if_expr`
#    note below).
#
# 4. Assignment targets are wy/wyrm node shapes, not pypoc's Target
#    dataclasses. pypoc distinguishes `NameTarget`/`VarTarget` (a plain
#    name), `AttrTarget` (an *attrs list* - `a.b.c` is one node with
#    `attrs=["b","c"]`) and `IndexTarget` (recursively wrapping a nested
#    target for `grid[i][j]`). wy/wyrm/parser.wy's `target` rule
#    (`wy/wyrm/ast.wy`) folds these into three shapes instead: a bare `sym`
#    (a name), a single-level `'attr` node (`a.b`; a longer `a.b.c` chain's
#    `base` field is a known parser quirk - see `ast.wy`'s
#    `$_mk_target_base`, not this port's to fix), and a `'target` node
#    (`base, [index, index, ...]`) carrying *every* trailing `[i][j]...`
#    in one flat list rather than pypoc's nested-IndexTarget nesting. So
#    `_target_base` below walks that flat list itself, in place of
#    pypoc's `_target_value`/`_target_base` recursive pair.
#
# 5. `var`/`:=` is two wy/wyrm kinds, not one. pypoc's `VarDecl.targets` is
#    always a list, whatever its length; wy/wyrm's parser instead emits a
#    single-target `'define` (`name, type, init`) or a multi-target
#    `'define_values` (`[[name, type], ...], init`) - see `ast.wy`. Both
#    route through the same `_assign` pairwise/from-call/unpacking core
#    pypoc's single `_var_decl` uses, just from two entry points
#    (`_define`/`_define_values`) instead of one.
#
# 6. `_do`'s stub is fixed here, closing the cycle M2 left open - but not
#    by an `import`. wy/wyrm's import graph must stay acyclic
#    (`pypoc/doc/addendum.md`, "Import cycles are illegal"), so
#    `expressions.wy` cannot `import wyrm::compiler::statements::*` the way
#    pypoc's `_do`/`_lambda` docstrings say their own function-local
#    `import` does. Instead `expressions.wy` now exposes
#    `set_body_compiler(compiler)`, a one-shot hook; this file calls
#    `set_body_compiler(compile_body)` at the bottom, once `compile_body`
#    exists. Anything that wants `do:` blocks to lower has to import
#    `wyrm::compiler::statements` (directly or transitively) before
#    compiling, so the hook is installed - which every caller of this
#    file's own `compile_body`/`compile_statement` already does by
#    definition. `_lambda` is untouched: M4's `functions.wy` needs the same
#    kind of hook (`compile_closure_expr`) and should add it the same way.
#
# 7. `nested_fn`/`defer` are stubs, on purpose. Both need `functions.wy`
#    (M4): a nested `fn`/`co` needs parameter/capture layout and a
#    table-index reservation (`compile_nested_fn`), and `defer` compiles
#    its block as a closure the same way (`compile_defer`). Registered here
#    so dispatch never silently falls through for them - `_nested_fn`/
#    `_defer` below answer a clear `error` naming exactly what is missing,
#    the same shape `_lambda`'s M2 stub takes.

import std::pairs::*
import wyrm::opcodes::*
import wyrm::compiler::context::*
import wyrm::compiler::analysis::*
import wyrm::compiler::expressions::*
import wyrm::compiler::handlers::*

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# small local helpers - see analysis.wy/expressions.wy's header comments for
# why these are copied rather than imported (no cross-module access to a
# leading-underscore name, and `std::pairs`' `list_tail`/`field` walk with a
# `for ... in range(...)`, which never iterates on this repo's C VM).

fn _tail(node, count: int):
    cur := node
    i := 0
    while i < count:
        if not (cur is pair):
            return error("index out of range")
        cur = cdr(cur)
        i = i + 1
    return cur

fn _field(node, index: int):
    rest := _tail(node, index)
    if rest is error:
        return rest
    return car(rest)

fn _pairlist(node) -> list:
    out := []
    p := node
    while p is pair:
        out ! append(car(p))
        p = cdr(p)
    return out

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# emit helpers - pack (or pack_pairable) THEN emit, checking the error each
# can return in one place. Copied from expressions.wy for the same reason
# `_field`/`_tail` are: no cross-module access to a leading-underscore name.

fn _emit_pack(frame: FnContext, op, f: int = 0, a0: int = 0, a1: int = 0, a2: int = 0, w1: int | nil = nil) -> nil | error:
    words := pack(op, f=f, a0=a0, a1=a1, a2=a2, w1=w1)
    if words is error:
        return words
    frame ! emit_words(words)
    return nil

fn _emit_pairable(frame: FnContext, op, primary: int = 0, secondary: int = 0) -> nil | error:
    words := pack_pairable(op, primary, secondary)
    if words is error:
        return words
    frame ! emit_words(words)
    return nil

# Constructs whose own lowering writes the statement's value; everything
# else is worth `lnil` when it lands in the value position.
VALUE_PRODUCING := ['expr_stmt, 'if, 'yield]

# Constructs that never fall through to the next statement, so a `lnil`
# ahead of one would be dead code.
NO_FALLTHROUGH := ['return, 'break, 'continue]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# entry points

fn compile_statement(node, frame: FnContext, result = nil):
    return dispatch(STATEMENT_HANDLERS, node, frame, result)

# Compile a statement list, releasing each statement's temps after it.
#
# `result`, when given, is the register the block's value lands in - the
# last statement's value, or nil if that statement has none.
#
# A statement list is also a scope: whatever the declaration pass allotted
# for it (see deviation 1) is in force here and gone afterwards, so an
# inner `var` shadows an outer name of the same spelling rather than
# writing through to it, and is not reachable once the block ends. A
# frame's own top-level body has no scope of its own to push -
# `FnContext.push_block` answers `false` for it, and nothing here pops what
# was never pushed.
fn compile_body(body, frame: FnContext, result = nil) -> nil | error:
    pushed := frame ! push_block(body)
    stmts := body_statements(body)
    n := len(stmts)
    i := 0
    failure := nil
    while (i < n) and (failure is nil):
        node := stmts[i]
        last := i == (n - 1)
        wants_value := last and not (result is nil)
        mark := frame ! mark()
        kind := node_kind(node)
        if wants_value and not (kind in VALUE_PRODUCING):
            if not (kind in NO_FALLTHROUGH):
                e := _emit_pack(frame, 'lnil, a0=result)
                if e is error:
                    failure = e
            if failure is nil:
                r := compile_statement(node, frame, nil)
                if r is error:
                    failure = r
        else:
            target := nil
            if wants_value:
                target = result
            r2 := compile_statement(node, frame, target)
            if r2 is error:
                failure = r2
        if failure is nil:
            fr := frame ! free_to(mark)
            if fr is error:
                failure = fr
        i = i + 1
    if pushed:
        frame ! pop_block()
    if not (failure is nil):
        return failure
    return nil

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# assignment targets

# The name a `var`/`:=` target binds - wy/wyrm's grammar only ever lets a
# `var_target` be a plain identifier (see ast.wy: `var_target ::=
# identifier, [":" , type_expression]`), so unlike `target` (below) this
# never needs to accept an `'attr`/`'target` shape.
fn target_name(target):
    if target is sym:
        return target
    return error("the bytecode compiler does not support " + construct_name(target) + " as a var name")

# Assign `value_node` to one target of any shape (spec 7.2).
fn store_target(target, value_node, frame: FnContext) -> nil | error:
    if target is sym:
        return store_expr(target, value_node, frame)
    kind := node_kind(target)
    if (kind == 'attr) or (kind == 'index) or (kind == 'target):
        return _store_through(target, value_node, frame)
    return error("the bytecode compiler does not support " + construct_name(target) + " as an assignment target")

# Assign a value that already sits in `reg` to one target.
fn store_target_reg(target, reg: int, frame: FnContext) -> nil | error:
    if target is sym:
        return store_reg(target, reg, frame)
    base := _target_base(target, frame)
    if base is error:
        return base
    return _emit_store(frame, base[0], base[1], base[2], base[3], reg)

fn _store_through(target, value_node, frame: FnContext) -> nil | error:
    base := _target_base(target, frame)
    if base is error:
        return base
    mark := frame ! mark()
    reg := frame ! push()
    if reg is error:
        return reg
    r := compile_expr(value_node, frame, reg)
    if r is error:
        return r
    e := _emit_store(frame, base[0], base[1], base[2], base[3], reg)
    if e is error:
        return e
    return frame ! free_to(mark)

fn _emit_store(frame: FnContext, obj, symbol, index, slotreg, reg) -> nil | error:
    if not (slotreg is nil):
        return _emit_pack(frame, 'setslot, a0=obj, a1=slotreg, a2=reg)
    if not (symbol is nil):
        return _emit_pack(frame, 'setattr, a0=obj, a1=symbol, a2=reg)
    return _emit_pack(frame, 'setidx, a0=obj, a1=index, a2=reg)

# Evaluate everything left of the final `.name`/`[i]` of a target.
#
# `a.b.c = v` reads `a.b` and stores into its `c`; `grid[i][j] = v` reads
# `grid[i]` and stores at `j`. Answers `[object register, symbol index or
# nil, index register or nil, slotreg number or nil]` - see deviation 4 for why
# this walks a flat index list instead of recursing through nested target
# nodes the way statements.py's `_target_base`/`_target_value` do.
fn _target_base(target, frame: FnContext) -> list | error:
    kind := node_kind(target)
    if kind == 'attr:
        obj_node := (_field(target, 1) catch nil)
        name := (_field(target, 2) catch nil)
        this_target := obj_node == 'this
        obj := compile_expr(obj_node, frame, nil)
        if obj is error:
            return obj
        if this_target and (not (frame.this_class is nil)) and frame.module.slot_optimization:
            slotreg := (frame.module) ! slot_index(frame.this_class, name)
            if not (slotreg is nil):
                return [obj, nil, nil, slotreg]
        symbol := (frame.module.image) ! add_symbol(str(name))
        if symbol is error:
            return symbol
        return [obj, symbol, nil, nil]
    if kind == 'index:
        obj_node := (_field(target, 1) catch nil)
        idx_node := (_field(target, 2) catch nil)
        obj := compile_expr(obj_node, frame, nil)
        if obj is error:
            return obj
        idx := compile_expr(idx_node, frame, nil)
        if idx is error:
            return idx
        return [obj, nil, idx, nil]
    if kind == 'target:
        base_node := (_field(target, 1) catch nil)
        idx_nodes := _pairlist((_field(target, 2) catch nil))
        obj := compile_expr(base_node, frame, nil)
        if obj is error:
            return obj
        n := len(idx_nodes)
        if n == 0:
            return error("assignment target has no index")
        i := 0
        while i < (n - 1):
            idx_reg := compile_expr(idx_nodes[i], frame, nil)
            if idx_reg is error:
                return idx_reg
            step := frame ! push()
            if step is error:
                return step
            e := _emit_pack(frame, 'getidx, a0=step, a1=obj, a2=idx_reg)
            if e is error:
                return e
            obj = step
            i = i + 1
        last_reg := compile_expr(idx_nodes[n - 1], frame, nil)
        if last_reg is error:
            return last_reg
        return [obj, nil, last_reg, nil]
    return error("the bytecode compiler does not support " + construct_name(target) + " as an assignment target")

# Evaluate `value_node` into whatever `name` names.
fn store_expr(name, value_node, frame: FnContext) -> nil | error:
    where := _storage(name, frame)
    if where is error:
        return where
    kind := where[0]
    slotreg := where[1]
    if (kind == "reg") and (not (name in frame.cells)):
        r := compile_expr(value_node, frame, slotreg)
        if r is error:
            return r
        return nil
    mark := frame ! mark()
    reg := frame ! push()
    if reg is error:
        return reg
    r2 := compile_expr(value_node, frame, reg)
    if r2 is error:
        return r2
    e := store_reg(name, reg, frame)
    if e is error:
        return e
    return frame ! free_to(mark)

# Put a value that already sits in `reg` into whatever `name` names.
fn store_reg(name, reg: int, frame: FnContext) -> nil | error:
    where := _storage(name, frame)
    if where is error:
        return where
    kind := where[0]
    slotreg := where[1]
    if kind == "slot":
        return _emit_pack(frame, 'setattr, a0=frame.this_reg, a1=slotreg, a2=reg)
    if kind == "global":
        return _emit_pairable(frame, 'gset, slotreg, reg)
    if name in frame.cells:
        # The slotreg holds the box; the write goes into it, so every frame
        # sharing the box sees it (spec 8.3).
        z := frame ! zero()
        if z is error:
            return z
        return _emit_pack(frame, 'setidx, a0=slotreg, a1=z, a2=reg)
    if slotreg != reg:
        return _emit_pairable(frame, 'move, slotreg, reg)
    return nil

fn _storage(name, frame: FnContext) -> list | error:
    where := frame ! storage(name)
    if where is nil:
        # A name this module does not own is a refusal (spec: a free slotreg
        # is this module's copy of a name, not a handle on the other
        # module's storage).
        return error("cannot assign to '" + str(name) + "': it is not declared in this module")
    return where

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# declarations

fn _var_decl_unset(names: list, frame: FnContext) -> nil | error:
    i := 0
    n := len(names)
    while i < n:
        e := _store_unset(names[i], frame)
        if e is error:
            return e
        i = i + 1
    return nil

# `var x: T` with no initializer - bound to Unset until first assignment.
fn _define(node, frame: FnContext, result):
    name := (_field(node, 1) catch nil)
    init := (_field(node, 3) catch nil)
    if init is nil:
        return _store_unset(name, frame)
    return _assign([name], [init], frame)

fn _define_values(node, frame: FnContext, result):
    pairs := (_field(node, 1) catch nil)
    init := (_field(node, 2) catch nil)
    names := []
    p := pairs
    while p is pair:
        entry := car(p)
        names ! append(car(entry))
        p = cdr(p)
    if init is nil:
        return _var_decl_unset(names, frame)
    return _assign(names, [init], frame)

fn _set_stmt(node, frame: FnContext, result):
    target := (_field(node, 1) catch nil)
    value := (_field(node, 2) catch nil)
    return _assign([target], [value], frame)

# `x ?= e`: assign only when x currently holds an error (spec 7.2). Only a
# plain name target is meaningful here - the parser already refuses more
# than one target (`ast.wy`'s `$_mk_assign`), and `target_name` refuses
# anything but a bare name.
fn _if_set_stmt(node, frame: FnContext, result):
    target_node := (_field(node, 1) catch nil)
    value := (_field(node, 2) catch nil)
    name := target_name(target_node)
    if name is error:
        return name
    return _set_if_unset(name, value, frame)

fn _set_values_stmt(node, frame: FnContext, result):
    targets_node := (_field(node, 1) catch nil)
    value := (_field(node, 2) catch nil)
    targets := _pairlist(targets_node)
    return _assign(targets, [value], frame)

fn _assign(targets: list, values: list, frame: FnContext) -> nil | error:
    if len(values) == len(targets):
        return _assign_pairwise(targets, values, frame)
    if len(values) != 1:
        return error(str(len(targets)) + " target(s) but " + str(len(values)) + " value(s)")
    value := values[0]
    if node_kind(value) == 'call:
        return _assign_from_call(targets, value, frame)
    if node_kind(value) == 'tuple:
        items := _pairlist((_tail(value, 1) catch nil))
        if len(items) == len(targets):
            return _assign_pairwise(targets, items, frame)
    return _assign_by_unpacking(targets, value, frame)

# Assign values to targets position by position.
#
# With more than one target every value is evaluated *before* any target
# is written, because the two sides may overlap - `a, b = b, a` is a swap,
# not two assignments in a row.
fn _assign_pairwise(targets: list, values: list, frame: FnContext) -> nil | error:
    if len(targets) == 1:
        return store_target(targets[0], values[0], frame)
    mark := frame ! mark()
    staged := []
    for value in values:
        reg := frame ! push()
        if reg is error:
            return reg
        r := compile_expr(value, frame, reg)
        if r is error:
            return r
        staged ! append(reg)
    i := 0
    n := len(targets)
    while i < n:
        e := store_target_reg(targets[i], staged[i], frame)
        if e is error:
            return e
        i = i + 1
    return frame ! free_to(mark)

# A call asked for as many results as there are targets: the VM backfills
# missing ones with nil and drops extras (spec 1), so the window is the
# whole mechanism.
fn _assign_from_call(targets: list, call_node, frame: FnContext) -> nil | error:
    base := frame ! mark()
    r := compile_call(call_node, frame, len(targets))
    if r is error:
        return r
    i := 0
    n := len(targets)
    while i < n:
        e := store_target_reg(targets[i], L(base + i), frame)
        if e is error:
            return e
        i = i + 1
    return frame ! free_to(base)

# `a, b = e`: pull exactly len(targets) items out of one indexable.
#
# On a length or type mismatch every destination register receives the
# error value (spec 3.3), so the failure shows up at each use rather than
# at the unpack.
fn _assign_by_unpacking(targets: list, value_node, frame: FnContext) -> nil | error:
    mark := frame ! mark()
    source := compile_expr(value_node, frame, nil)
    if source is error:
        return source
    base := frame ! mark()
    n := len(targets)
    i := 0
    while i < n:
        p := frame ! push()
        if p is error:
            return p
        i = i + 1
    e := _emit_pack(frame, 'unpack, a0=L(base), a1=source, f=n)
    if e is error:
        return e
    i = 0
    while i < n:
        e2 := store_target_reg(targets[i], L(base + i), frame)
        if e2 is error:
            return e2
        i = i + 1
    return frame ! free_to(mark)

fn _store_unset(name, frame: FnContext) -> nil | error:
    where := _storage(name, frame)
    if where is error:
        return where
    kind := where[0]
    slotreg := where[1]
    if (kind == "reg") and (not (name in frame.cells)):
        return _emit_pack(frame, 'lunset, a0=slotreg)
    mark := frame ! mark()
    reg := frame ! push()
    if reg is error:
        return reg
    e := _emit_pack(frame, 'lunset, a0=reg)
    if e is error:
        return e
    e2 := store_reg(name, reg, frame)
    if e2 is error:
        return e2
    return frame ! free_to(mark)

fn _set_if_unset(name, value_node, frame: FnContext) -> nil | error:
    where := _storage(name, frame)
    if where is error:
        return where
    kind := where[0]
    slotreg := where[1]
    done := frame ! new_label()
    if (kind == "reg") and (not (name in frame.cells)):
        e := frame ! emit_jump('jnerr, done, slotreg)
        if e is error:
            return e
        mark := frame ! mark()
        r := compile_expr(value_node, frame, slotreg)
        if r is error:
            return r
        fe := frame ! free_to(mark)
        if fe is error:
            return fe
    elif kind == "reg":
        mark := frame ! mark()
        reg := frame ! push()
        if reg is error:
            return reg
        z := frame ! zero()
        if z is error:
            return z
        e := _emit_pack(frame, 'getidx, a0=reg, a1=slotreg, a2=z)
        if e is error:
            return e
        e2 := frame ! emit_jump('jnerr, done, reg)
        if e2 is error:
            return e2
        r2 := compile_expr(value_node, frame, reg)
        if r2 is error:
            return r2
        e3 := _emit_pack(frame, 'setidx, a0=slotreg, a1=z, a2=reg)
        if e3 is error:
            return e3
        fe2 := frame ! free_to(mark)
        if fe2 is error:
            return fe2
    else:
        mark := frame ! mark()
        reg := frame ! push()
        if reg is error:
            return reg
        e := _emit_pairable(frame, 'gget, slotreg, reg)
        if e is error:
            return e
        e2 := frame ! emit_jump('jnerr, done, reg)
        if e2 is error:
            return e2
        r2 := compile_expr(value_node, frame, reg)
        if r2 is error:
            return r2
        e3 := _emit_pairable(frame, 'gset, slotreg, reg)
        if e3 is error:
            return e3
        fe := frame ! free_to(mark)
        if fe is error:
            return fe
    return frame ! mark_label(done)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# simple statements

fn _pass(node, frame: FnContext, result):
    return nil

fn _expr_stmt(node, frame: FnContext, result):
    value := (_field(node, 1) catch nil)
    return compile_expr(value, frame, result)

# `return e` returns one value from wherever it landed; `return a, b`
# returns a window of them; bare `return` returns a single nil, so a
# caller asking for one result gets nil rather than a zero-value backfill.
fn _return(node, frame: FnContext, result):
    value := (_field(node, 1) catch nil)
    if value is nil:
        reg := frame ! push()
        if reg is error:
            return reg
        e := _emit_pack(frame, 'lnil, a0=reg)
        if e is error:
            return e
        return frame ! emit_return(reg)
    if node_kind(value) == 'tuple:
        # `return 1, 2` and `return (1, 2)` are the same tree, so a
        # parenthesized tuple return is lowered as a multi-value return.
        base := frame ! mark()
        items := _pairlist((_tail(value, 1) catch nil))
        for item in items:
            w := compile_into_window(item, frame)
            if w is error:
                return w
        count := len(items)
        if count > 128:
            return error(str(count) + " return values, over the 128-result limit")
        if count > frame.max_results:
            frame.max_results = count
        return frame ! emit_return(L(base), count)
    reg := compile_expr(value, frame, nil)
    if reg is error:
        return reg
    return frame ! emit_return(reg)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# control flow

fn _if_lower(node, frame: FnContext, result) -> nil | error:
    cond := (_field(node, 1) catch nil)
    body := (_field(node, 2) catch nil)
    elifs := (_field(node, 3) catch nil)
    orelse := (_field(node, 4) catch nil)
    clauses := [[cond, body]]
    p := elifs
    while p is pair:
        clause := car(p)
        clauses ! append([car(clause), (cadr(clause) catch nil)])
        p = cdr(p)
    end := frame ! new_label()
    if (not (result is nil)) and (orelse is nil):
        # Nothing may run at all, and a skipped `if` is nil (spec 7.2).
        e := _emit_pack(frame, 'lnil, a0=result)
        if e is error:
            return e
    n := len(clauses)
    i := 0
    while i < n:
        last := i == (n - 1)
        skip := frame ! new_label()
        mark := frame ! mark()
        cond_reg := compile_expr(clauses[i][0], frame, nil)
        if cond_reg is error:
            return cond_reg
        e2 := frame ! emit_jump('jf, skip, cond_reg)
        if e2 is error:
            return e2
        fe := frame ! free_to(mark)
        if fe is error:
            return fe
        cb := compile_body(clauses[i][1], frame, result)
        if cb is error:
            return cb
        if (not last) or (not (orelse is nil)):
            e3 := frame ! emit_jump('jmp, end)
            if e3 is error:
                return e3
        ml := frame ! mark_label(skip)
        if ml is error:
            return ml
        i = i + 1
    if not (orelse is nil):
        oe := compile_body(orelse, frame, result)
        if oe is error:
            return oe
    return frame ! mark_label(end)

fn _if_stmt(node, frame: FnContext, result):
    return _if_lower(node, frame, result)

# `if` is an expression as well as a statement (wyrm.gram's `if_expr`), and
# it is the same node either way - so it is the same lowering, with the
# branch values landing in the register the expression was asked for.
#
# Registered in EXPR_HANDLERS for completeness (per this milestone's scope)
# but not yet reachable: expressions.wy's `compile_expr` dispatches with
# its own hand-written `if ks == ...` chain (M2's idiom) rather than
# consulting `EXPR_HANDLERS`, and that chain has no `"if"` case. Wiring it
# up needs one line added to that chain, which is expressions.wy code this
# milestone's scope does not touch (see the epic report).
fn _if_expr(node, frame: FnContext, dst):
    reg := dst
    if reg is nil:
        r := frame ! push()
        if r is error:
            return r
        reg = r
    e := _if_lower(node, frame, reg)
    if e is error:
        return e
    return reg

fn _while(node, frame: FnContext, result) -> nil | error:
    cond := (_field(node, 1) catch nil)
    body := (_field(node, 2) catch nil)
    head := frame ! new_label()
    end := frame ! new_label()
    ml := frame ! mark_label(head)
    if ml is error:
        return ml
    mark := frame ! mark()
    cond_reg := compile_expr(cond, frame, nil)
    if cond_reg is error:
        return cond_reg
    e := frame ! emit_jump('jf, end, cond_reg)
    if e is error:
        return e
    fe := frame ! free_to(mark)
    if fe is error:
        return fe
    (frame.loops) ! append([head, end])
    cb := compile_body(body, frame, nil)
    (frame.loops) ! resize(len(frame.loops) - 1)
    if cb is error:
        return cb
    e2 := frame ! emit_jump('jmp, head)
    if e2 is error:
        return e2
    return frame ! mark_label(end)

# `for x in e / else`: `iter` once, then `itnext` per turn, which jumps to
# the else clause when the iterator is exhausted (spec 7.2). `break` jumps
# *past* the else clause; exhaustion jumps *to* it.
#
# The loop variable is a declaration scoped to the loop, not to the block
# the loop sits in: it is bound for the body and for the else clause (which
# reads the final iteration's value), and out of scope afterwards - so the
# scope pushed here is the loop's own, and the body pushes its own inside
# it (analysis.wy's `block_scopes` allots the loop node itself as a scope
# owner for exactly this reason).
fn _for(node, frame: FnContext, result) -> nil | error:
    pushed := frame ! push_block(node)
    outcome := _compile_for(node, frame)
    if pushed:
        frame ! pop_block()
    return outcome

fn _compile_for(node, frame: FnContext) -> nil | error:
    var_name := (_field(node, 1) catch nil)
    iter_node := (_field(node, 2) catch nil)
    body := (_field(node, 3) catch nil)
    orelse := (_field(node, 4) catch nil)
    mark := frame ! mark()
    source := compile_expr(iter_node, frame, nil)
    if source is error:
        return source
    iterator := frame ! push()
    if iterator is error:
        return iterator
    e := _emit_pack(frame, 'iter, a0=iterator, a1=source)
    if e is error:
        return e
    where := _storage(var_name, frame)
    if where is error:
        return where
    kind := where[0]
    slotreg := where[1]
    var target: int
    if kind == "reg":
        target = slotreg
    else:
        t := frame ! push()
        if t is error:
            return t
        target = t
    head := frame ! new_label()
    exhausted := frame ! new_label()
    end := frame ! new_label()
    mh := frame ! mark_label(head)
    if mh is error:
        return mh
    words := pack('itnext, a0=target, a1=iterator, a2=0)
    if words is error:
        return words
    offset := frame ! emit_words(words)
    frame ! patch_site(exhausted, offset + 2, offset + 1, "a2")
    if kind == "global":
        eg := _emit_pairable(frame, 'gset, slotreg, target)
        if eg is error:
            return eg
    (frame.loops) ! append([head, end])
    cb := compile_body(body, frame, nil)
    (frame.loops) ! resize(len(frame.loops) - 1)
    if cb is error:
        return cb
    ej := frame ! emit_jump('jmp, head)
    if ej is error:
        return ej
    mx := frame ! mark_label(exhausted)
    if mx is error:
        return mx
    if not (orelse is nil):
        oe := compile_body(orelse, frame, nil)
        if oe is error:
            return oe
    me := frame ! mark_label(end)
    if me is error:
        return me
    return frame ! free_to(mark)

fn _break(node, frame: FnContext, result):
    if len(frame.loops) == 0:
        return error("`break` outside a loop")
    top := frame.loops[len(frame.loops) - 1]
    return frame ! emit_jump('jmp, top[1])

fn _continue(node, frame: FnContext, result):
    if len(frame.loops) == 0:
        return error("`continue` outside a loop")
    top := frame.loops[len(frame.loops) - 1]
    return frame ! emit_jump('jmp, top[0])

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# nested definitions and deferred blocks

fn _yield_stmt(node, frame: FnContext, result):
    return compile_yield(node, frame, result)

# A `static` declaration allots a module global and its initializer runs
# once, in module init, where its owner is created - so nothing is emitted
# where the declaration itself appears (spec 7.2). `frame.statics` is
# populated by the function/class prologue (M4/M6), not here; this handler
# only checks that it was.
fn _static_decl(node, frame: FnContext, result):
    name := (_field(node, 1) catch nil)
    if not (name in frame.statics):
        return error("`static` is only meaningful inside a function or class body")
    return nil

# M4 closes this the same way deviation 6 closes `_do`'s: a one-shot hook,
# not an `import` - functions.wy needs `compile_body`/`store_reg` from
# *this* file, so this file cannot import functions.wy back without
# breaking wy/wyrm's required-acyclic import graph. `functions.wy` calls
# `set_nested_fn_compiler(compile_nested_fn)` /
# `set_defer_compiler(compile_defer)` at its own bottom, once those exist;
# anything that wants a nested `fn`/`co` or a `defer` block to lower has to
# import `wyrm::compiler::functions` (directly or transitively) before
# compiling, which installs both hooks as a side effect of that import.
_nested_fn_compiler := nil
_defer_compiler := nil

fn set_nested_fn_compiler(compiler) -> nil:
    _nested_fn_compiler = compiler
    return nil

fn set_defer_compiler(compiler) -> nil:
    _defer_compiler = compiler
    return nil

# `result` (this statement's value slot, if any) is unused in both: a
# nested `fn`/`co` definition and a `defer` block are declarations, not
# expressions, and read as nil in value position like any other
# (compile_body's VALUE_PRODUCING list does not include either kind, so a
# caller never passes a non-nil result here).
fn _nested_fn(node, frame: FnContext, result):
    if _nested_fn_compiler is nil:
        return error("nested `fn`/`co` definitions need functions.wy's compile_nested_fn (M4); import wyrm::compiler::functions before compiling a module with nested definitions")
    return _nested_fn_compiler(node, frame, nil)

fn _defer(node, frame: FnContext, result):
    if _defer_compiler is nil:
        return error("`defer` needs functions.wy's compile_defer (M4); import wyrm::compiler::functions before compiling a module with `defer`")
    return _defer_compiler(node, frame)

# A `'template` wrapper around a statement (predefined.wy's rewrite of
# `@template X`, epic 10a M0): a nested definition compiles with the
# marking handed down - its body may stub instead of failing the module -
# and anything else compiles exactly as written, because the marking only
# relieves a *lowering failure*; it does not change what the statement
# means. (The toplevel counterpart lives in module.wy, which owns the
# definition-entry paths; this is the same handler one frame down.)
fn _template_stmt(node, frame: FnContext, result):
    inner := (_field(node, 1) catch nil)
    kind := node_kind(inner)
    if (kind == 'fn_def) or (kind == 'co_def):
        if _nested_fn_compiler is nil:
            return error("nested `fn`/`co` definitions need functions.wy's compile_nested_fn (M4); import wyrm::compiler::functions before compiling a module with nested definitions")
        return _nested_fn_compiler(inner, frame, nil, true)
    return compile_statement(inner, frame, result)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# registration - handlers.wy's registries, populated at module load

register_statement(['define], _define)
register_statement(['define_values], _define_values)
register_statement(['set], _set_stmt)
register_statement(['if_set], _if_set_stmt)
register_statement(['set_values], _set_values_stmt)
register_statement(['pass], _pass)
register_statement(['expr_stmt], _expr_stmt)
register_statement(['return], _return)
register_statement(['if], _if_stmt)
register_statement(['while], _while)
register_statement(['for], _for)
register_statement(['break], _break)
register_statement(['continue], _continue)
register_statement(['yield], _yield_stmt)
register_statement(['static], _static_decl)
register_statement(['fn_def, 'co_def], _nested_fn)
register_statement(['defer], _defer)
register_statement(['template], _template_stmt)

register_expression(['if], _if_expr)

# Close the `_do` loop (deviation 6): expressions.wy's `'do` handler calls
# through this hook once it is installed.
set_body_compiler(compile_body)
